1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,820
A coronary angiogram answers a question:
is there a narrowing or blockage in an

2
00:00:05,825 --> 00:00:08,377
artery that supplies the heart muscle?

3
00:00:08,468 --> 00:00:12,413
The heart muscle gets its blood supply
from the coronary arteries.

4
00:00:12,694 --> 00:00:17,486
Plaque can build up in an artery wall and
make the channel narrower.

5
00:00:17,953 --> 00:00:22,618
An angiogram may be planned after a review
of symptoms and other tests.

6
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:28,583
In some suspected heart attacks, the team
may need an urgent angiogram and may go on

7
00:00:28,588 --> 00:00:30,246
to treat the artery.

8
00:00:30,635 --> 00:00:35,297
For a planned angiogram, your team
explains the test and reviews your

9
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:39,898
medicines, kidney health and any previous
reaction to contrast.

10
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:44,588
Follow their instructions about food,
drink and medicines.

11
00:00:44,702 --> 00:00:48,101
The radial artery runs on the thumb side
of the wrist.

12
00:00:48,475 --> 00:00:53,616
Here the team checks the right wrist and
uses ultrasound to see the artery.

13
00:00:53,735 --> 00:00:59,411
Once the area is numb, a small access
needle enters the radial artery.

14
00:00:59,502 --> 00:01:05,293
A short access wire passes through the
hollow needle and into the radial artery.

15
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:07,801
The wire points up the arm.

16
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:10,918
The needle slides back out over the wire.

17
00:01:11,291 --> 00:01:15,782
Watch how the wire stays in the artery and
preserves the entry path.

18
00:01:16,168 --> 00:01:19,777
The short radial sheath goes in over the
access wire.

19
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:25,860
A tapered insert, called a dilator, sits
inside the sheath as it goes in.

20
00:01:26,235 --> 00:01:30,424
The team removes the short access wire and
the dilator.

21
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:33,256
The outer sheath stays at the wrist.

22
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:36,844
It gives the next instruments a way into
the artery.

23
00:01:37,202 --> 00:01:41,228
A longer diagnostic guidewire passes
through the sheath.

24
00:01:41,811 --> 00:01:47,401
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, lets the
cardiologist see where the wire is

25
00:01:47,406 --> 00:01:52,840
going. The diagnostic catheter advances
over the long guidewire.

26
00:01:52,854 --> 00:01:56,267
The short sheath remains where the team
entered the artery.

27
00:01:56,302 --> 00:02:00,744
From the wrist, the wire and catheter
follow arteries up the arm, through the

28
00:02:00,749 --> 00:02:04,834
shoulder, and into the large artery that
leads to the aorta.

29
00:02:04,968 --> 00:02:10,294
The common femoral artery runs beneath the
skin in the upper thigh near the groin.

30
00:02:10,435 --> 00:02:15,666
After numbing the site, the cardiologist
uses a fine access needle to enter the

31
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:18,804
common femoral artery under ultrasound.

32
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:22,753
A fine access wire passes through the
hollow needle.

33
00:02:23,126 --> 00:02:28,453
X-ray helps the team confirm that the wire
follows the main iliac artery.

34
00:02:28,935 --> 00:02:31,998
The needle slides back out over the wire.

35
00:02:32,102 --> 00:02:37,333
The team passes a temporary micropuncture
introducer with its inner dilator over the

36
00:02:37,338 --> 00:02:42,250
fine wire. The fine wire and inner dilator
are taken out.

37
00:02:42,624 --> 00:02:47,951
The outer introducer stays in the artery
so the team can exchange wires.

38
00:02:48,335 --> 00:02:53,928
A larger exchange wire goes through the
temporary introducer and into the artery.

39
00:02:54,402 --> 00:02:58,417
The temporary introducer comes out over
the exchange wire.

40
00:02:58,791 --> 00:03:01,238
The exchange wire stays in place.

41
00:03:01,602 --> 00:03:06,859
The working femoral sheath and its dilator
slide in over the exchange wire.

42
00:03:07,235 --> 00:03:10,704
The team removes the exchange wire and the
dilator.

43
00:03:10,985 --> 00:03:13,572
The working sheath stays at the groin.

44
00:03:13,725 --> 00:03:17,775
The team can take a separate groin image
to check the entry site.

45
00:03:17,835 --> 00:03:23,112
The wire and catheter travel through the
right external and common iliac arteries,

46
00:03:23,117 --> 00:03:26,308
then up the abdominal and chest aorta.

47
00:03:26,774 --> 00:03:31,669
They round the aortic arch toward the
aortic root, where the coronary arteries

48
00:03:31,674 --> 00:03:34,504
begin above the aortic valve.

49
00:03:34,735 --> 00:03:39,505
At the aortic root, the long guidewire is
withdrawn from the catheter.

50
00:03:39,716 --> 00:03:45,434
Here the curved catheter tip is positioned
at the left coronary opening, above the

51
00:03:45,439 --> 00:03:46,830
aortic valve.

52
00:03:47,202 --> 00:03:52,273
The cardiologist injects X-ray contrast
through the diagnostic catheter.

53
00:03:52,647 --> 00:03:56,674
For a moment, it outlines the channel that
blood travels through.

54
00:03:57,047 --> 00:04:02,046
Watch the left main artery fill and then
see two of its main branches come into

55
00:04:02,051 --> 00:04:06,240
view. Now look at the same moment on the
X-ray.

56
00:04:06,614 --> 00:04:10,501
The dark branching lines show where the
contrast is flowing.

57
00:04:10,968 --> 00:04:16,260
In this groin example, the team uses a
different catheter for the right artery.

58
00:04:16,634 --> 00:04:21,531
The left catheter moves back from the
coronary opening and is withdrawn.

59
00:04:21,997 --> 00:04:24,701
The working sheath stays at the groin.

60
00:04:24,802 --> 00:04:29,432
The right diagnostic catheter follows a
guidewire up through the aorta.

61
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:34,410
The team withdraws the wire and positions
the catheter tip at the right coronary

62
00:04:34,415 --> 00:04:40,078
opening. The cardiologist injects contrast
separately at the right coronary

63
00:04:40,083 --> 00:04:46,234
opening. The right coronary artery and its
branches briefly appear on X-ray.

64
00:04:46,701 --> 00:04:50,550
The imaging arm can move around the
patient to record the arteries from

65
00:04:50,555 --> 00:04:51,935
different angles.

66
00:04:52,302 --> 00:04:57,362
On this example image, the contrast-filled
channel is thinner at one spot.

67
00:04:57,468 --> 00:05:02,142
The next step depends on the findings, the
symptoms, and whether the situation is

68
00:05:02,147 --> 00:05:07,854
urgent. It may be medicines, another test,
angioplasty, or a discussion about

69
00:05:07,859 --> 00:05:09,494
bypass surgery.

70
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:15,229
Angioplasty uses a balloon and may place a
stent to treat a narrowing.

71
00:05:15,603 --> 00:05:19,537
In selected cases the team can do that in
the same session.

72
00:05:19,602 --> 00:05:23,765
After the images are complete, the
cardiologist withdraws the diagnostic

73
00:05:23,770 --> 00:05:25,660
catheter through the sheath.

74
00:05:25,871 --> 00:05:28,200
The sheath is still at the access site.

75
00:05:28,235 --> 00:05:33,060
For manual closure, the team removes the
groin sheath and presses firmly over the

76
00:05:33,065 --> 00:05:35,547
entry site to control bleeding.

77
00:05:36,002 --> 00:05:41,595
In selected cases, the team uses a closure
device, such as a plug or stitch system.

78
00:05:41,807 --> 00:05:45,914
Staff check the site and leg circulation
before the patient gets up.

79
00:05:46,302 --> 00:05:51,013
Staff check your pulse, blood pressure,
groin and leg circulation.

80
00:05:51,294 --> 00:05:55,657
You may need to lie flat with the leg
straight while the site is monitored.

81
00:05:55,768 --> 00:06:01,025
Staff position a wrist compression band
over the puncture as the sheath comes out.

82
00:06:01,608 --> 00:06:04,900
The band puts pressure over the site to
control bleeding.

83
00:06:04,935 --> 00:06:08,672
Staff check the puncture site and
circulation in the hand.

84
00:06:08,952 --> 00:06:13,443
They gradually reduce the band pressure
and remove it when it is safe.

85
00:06:13,556 --> 00:06:19,138
After an uncomplicated planned test, many
patients can go home the same day.

86
00:06:19,419 --> 00:06:21,913
Others need longer observation.

87
00:06:22,035 --> 00:06:26,605
Follow the written instructions for the
dressing, activity, medicines and

88
00:06:26,610 --> 00:06:27,652
follow-up.

89
00:06:28,235 --> 00:06:31,496
A small bruise or mild soreness can
happen.

90
00:06:31,869 --> 00:06:36,659
Contact the care team promptly for
increasing redness or pain, drainage or

91
00:06:36,664 --> 00:06:37,637
fever.

92
00:06:38,235 --> 00:06:42,711
Call local emergency services for heavy
bleeding or bleeding that continues

93
00:06:42,716 --> 00:06:48,670
despite firm pressure, rapidly growing
swelling, a newly cold or numb hand,

94
00:06:48,823 --> 00:06:52,072
new chest pressure or sudden shortness of
breath.

95
00:06:52,169 --> 00:06:56,576
Before you go home, make sure you know
your result and what to do during

96
00:06:56,581 --> 00:06:57,786
recovery.
